### Executive Summary

A Rung-3 witness probe and mutant kill table have been built, measured, and verified for the ARM block data transfer (`armbdt`) unaligned address defect on the unmodified in-tree ARM machine `testarm` (`-V -A -T -E testarm -M 64`). All work was strictly isolated in the scratch clone `/tmp/gxarmbdt_clone`. The live regression running in `/tmp/gxop519p2` and all other reserved workspaces were untouched.

1. **Root Cause**:
   In [`cpu_arm_instr.c`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c), block transfers evaluate host page cache residency per register:
   - **Fast path** (`page != NULL`): accesses `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`. The `>> 2` truncates bits `[1:0]`, which is architecturally correct per ARM DDI 0100I A4-37 / A4-190 (*"ignore least significant two bits"*).
   - **Slow path** (cold page, `page == NULL`): calls `cpu->memory_rw()` at line 1653 (`arm_pop`) and line 1842 (`arm_push`), passing the **raw, unmasked `addr`**. In [`memory_rw.c:728-782`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/memory_rw.c#L728-L782), `offset = paddr & offset_mask` indexes at page granularity (not word-rounded), performing raw `memcpy` across word boundaries.
   - **The Core Symptom**: An unaligned base register transfers **different bytes** depending purely on host page cache warmth—a condition invisible to the guest architecture.
2. **Fix Shape vs. Mask-at-Entry Trap**:
   - The fix masks strictly at the two `memory_rw` call sites:
     ```c
     cpu_arm_instr.c:1653: if (!cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, addr & ~3, data, sizeof(data), MEM_READ, CACHE_DATA))
     cpu_arm_instr.c:1842: if (!cpu->memory_rw(cpu,